      "funding_type": "grant",
      "typical_award_size": "The County applied for Homekey+ in 2025 with Restorative Partners for the Healing and Restoration Campus (HARC) on Los Osos Valley Road, permanent supportive housing for justice-involved people at risk of homelessness; as of the March 10, 2026 annual report no award notice had been received, and no County of San Luis Obispo project appears on HCD's Homekey+ awardee list dated October 14, 2026. Separately the County is advancing Welcome Home Village (40 permanent supportive housing units and 14 interim shelter units, anticipated opening spring 2026) with HHAP and County funds. The County's FY 2025/26 contracts for services, housing and facilities totaled $13,646,539.60. Earlier Homekey: City of San Luis Obispo Calle Joaquin (76 units, $19.4 million Homekey plus $2.6 million County SB 1090).",
      "per_unit_cap": "Homekey+ caps apply if awarded.",
      "affordability_requirement": "Permanent supportive housing for people experiencing homelessness with behavioral health needs or veterans.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "supportive housing",
        "interim housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "closed/inactive",
      "next_deadline": "Homekey+ NOFA funds are fully committed statewide; Welcome Home Village financing is still being assembled. Contact HSD for partnership opportunities.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "San Luis Obispo County's Department of Social Services Homeless Services Division (HSD, Devin Drake, Director; George Solis, Principal Administrative Manager; HSDgrants@co.slo.ca.us) runs one combined annual Action Plan NOFA each fall for CDBG, HOME, ESG, HHAP, PLHA, Title 29 and County General Fund Support on the Neighborly portal, and is the Continuum of Care collaborative applicant and administrative entity (CA-614). The Department of Planning and Building runs Title 22 land use incentives and assesses the new Title 31 in-lieu fee; the Behavioral Health Department holds BHSA; the Housing Authority of San Luis Obispo (HASLO) and its San Luis Obispo Nonprofit Housing Corporation, Paso Robles Housing Authority and Peoples' Self-Help Housing are the main developers; and the private nonprofit San Luis Obispo County Housing Trust Fund is the local CDFI gap lender. The Urban County covers the unincorporated area plus Arroyo Grande, Atascadero, Morro Bay, Paso Robles, Pismo Beach and San Luis Obispo (Grover Beach is outside). The County repealed its inclusionary ordinance on June 7, 2022 and replaced it in October 2025 with a voluntary Regional Housing Incentive Program and Regional Housing Fund. slocounty.ca.gov opens directly but rate-limits bursts (HTTP 429); Legistar web API is not enabled for the County. The County has not yet landed a Homekey+ award for HARC; Welcome Home Village is a County-led interim and PSH campus funded with HHAP, General Fund and state homelessness grants. A developer or operator with PSH experience could partner on this or on a future scattered-site application.",
      "source_url": "https://www.slocounty.ca.gov/departments/social-services/forms-documents/homeless-services/homepage/2026/2025-year-end-report",
      "record_id": "d56094870b",
      "cycle_notes": "",
